<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Tefilas Arvis [Laws of the Evening Prayer]<br>Halachah 4, Part 1 (§§ 1–9)<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400">And in it will be explained the matter of Rosh HaShanah and the Ten Days of Teshuvah, Yom Kippur, Sukkos and the Four Species, Hoshana Rabbah, Shemini Atzeres and Simchas Torah</span><br>Based on <span class="lr">LM I:65</span> (<i>"Vayomer Boaz el Rus"</i>), see there the entire Torah from beginning to end</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 1                                   -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 1</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="h">Tefilas Arvis</span> that Yaakov Avinu, peace upon him, established — he who is the choicest of the <span class="h">Avos</span>. And it is the first prayer of all the three prayers of the day, for the day follows the night, and first comes night and then day, as explained in the words of our Sages of blessed memory. It emerges that <span class="h">Tefilas Arvis</span> is the first of the three prayers. For the essential perfection of prayer is specifically through the <span class="h">bitul</span> [self-nullification] by which one is included in the aspect of <span class="mx">"it is entirely one, entirely good."</span> Through this one merits to make one unity out of the entire prayer. And when one stands at the end of the prayer, one will still remember the first word of the prayer etc. — as explained in the Torah mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">For when a person stands to pray, he walks in the Supernal Field and gathers blossoms and flowers, roses and beautiful grasses etc. And immediately when he utters the first letter etc., how much more so when he completes one word — the word seizes the soul from which the speech emerges and embraces and grasps the soul with immense love, and does not allow the soul to proceed further etc. But this is not the perfection of prayer, for one must complete the entire prayer and then say additional songs, praises, supplications and requests etc. Therefore the essential perfection of prayer is only when one merits to make one unity out of the entire prayer. Meaning: even when one proceeds from this utterance and speaks other utterances, nevertheless one will never forget the first utterance. And even when one stands at the end of the prayer, one will still remember even the first utterance of the prayer. Until even in the last word of the prayer one will still be standing in the first word of the prayer — for it is entirely one etc. See there. And this one merits only through meriting to gaze upon the <span class="h">tachlis</span> [ultimate purpose], which is entirely one, entirely good etc. See there. And to gaze upon the <span class="h">tachlis</span> is impossible except through <span class="h">bitul</span>, through closing one's eyes completely — meaning one must shut one's eyes from the vision of this world completely, until one comes to the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span>. And then one will be included in the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, which is entirely one, entirely good. And through this one will merit to make one unity out of the entire prayer — which is the essential perfection of prayer as mentioned above. See there, all of this, well.</div>

<div class="p">And it is explained there that this is the aspect of what is embedded in human nature: that when one has great suffering, G-d forbid, one shuts and closes one's eyes very forcefully. For there, when one gazes upon the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, one sees that there is no suffering or evil in the world at all. For everything is for good. For the ultimate purpose of suffering is very great good. But it is impossible to gaze upon the <span class="h">tachlis</span> except through closing the eyes etc. For when one wishes to see something far from one's eyes, one must focus the vision etc. Similarly, when one wishes to gaze upon the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, which is very far from human eyes, one must close one's eyes completely and press them with one's finger — meaning one must close one's eyes from the vision of this world completely. And then specifically one can gaze upon the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, which is entirely one, entirely good, and there all suffering is entirely nullified, as mentioned above. Therefore it is embedded in human nature to close one's eyes forcefully during severe suffering — in order to flee from the suffering. For through closing one's eyes during suffering, through this one is included in the <span class="h">tachlis</span> where all suffering is nullified, as mentioned above. But it is impossible to remain there, for one would be nullified from existence, and necessarily the <span class="h">bitul</span> must be in the aspect of <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span> [running and returning]. And when one returns from the <span class="h">bitul</span>, the suffering returns and intensifies even more, G-d forbid, etc. But afterward, one cools the suffering through the Torah that one draws from the aspect of the <span class="h">bitul</span>. For through the <span class="h">bitul</span> one is included in the aspect of "entirely one, entirely good," and there one sees that all suffering is great good. And through this, joy is drawn upon him. And then, when one returns from the <span class="h">bitul</span> and a <span class="h">reshimah</span> [trace] remains from the <span class="h">bitul</span>, the joy becomes a vessel for receiving the Torah. And one merits attainment of the Torah through the light of the shining and sparkling of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> from the <span class="h">bitul</span>, which illuminates and shines into the mind that has become vessels for receiving the Torah through the joy etc. See there, all of this, well.</div>

<div class="p">And it is explained there that all this is accomplished through the <span class="h">Ba'al HaSadeh</span> [Master of the Field] who is engaged in the tikun of the Supernal Field where all the souls grow — who is engaged in bringing everything to the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, that they should be included and nullified in <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span> in the aspect of "entirely one, entirely good." Through this one merits to make one unity out of the entire prayer — which is the essential perfection of prayer, as mentioned above. See there, all of this, finely and well.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 2                                   -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 2</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="h">Tefilas Arvis</span>. For at night the judgments begin to intensify — <span class="mx">"and then all the executioners of the law go forth"</span> etc., as explained in the holy Zohar. One must flee from them and nullify them. Therefore one must then close one's eyes completely, in order to be included and nullified in the aspect of "entirely one, entirely good," where all suffering and judgments are nullified. And when one merits to be included there, through this is the essential perfection of prayer. For through being included in the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, through this one can make one unity out of the entire prayer, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="h">Tefilas Arvis</span> that Yaakov established — which is the first prayer of all the three prayers. For then, at night, from the great intensity of the judgments, may the Merciful One save us, that are aroused then, one must nullify oneself completely. Meaning: close one's eyes and be included in the <span class="h">tachlis</span> — this is the aspect of the darkness of night, the aspect of sleep, which is the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span>. And through the <span class="h">bitul</span> toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, through this is the essential tikun of prayer — meaning to make one unity out of the entire prayer, as mentioned above. Therefore <span class="h">Tefilas Arvis</span> is the first of the three prayers of the day. For through <span class="h">Tefilas Arvis</span> one draws the tikun of prayer as mentioned above — for then, at night, from the intensity of the judgments, may the Merciful One save us, one is included in the <span class="h">tachlis</span> through closing the eyes — which is entirely one, entirely good — from where the essential tikun of prayer derives, as mentioned above. And this is what is brought in the Writings of the Ari"zal: that during every <span class="h">Tefilas Arvis</span> one should close one's eyes from beginning to end — see there. For then, during <span class="h">Tefilas Arvis</span>, is the aspect of closing the eyes, in order to be included in the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, in order to nullify the judgments that intensify then, as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 3                                   -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 3</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>. For <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> is the great and awesome Day of Judgment. And then, from the intensity of the judgment that is aroused, one must nullify oneself completely in the aspect mentioned above. Until one comes to the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span>, to be included in <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>, and to gaze upon the <span class="h">tachlis</span> — through which all judgments are nullified, as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of the sleep and <span class="h">dormita</span> [deep slumber] that is brought in the Kavanos [of the Ari"zal] regarding <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> — that a deep slumber falls upon <span class="h">Ze'ir Anpin</span> etc., see there. This is the aspect of the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above. For sleep is the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span>: one removes and nullifies one's <span class="h">da'as</span> and is included in the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="h">Olam HaBa</span>, which is the aspect of the light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>. And there, when one merits to be included there and to gaze upon the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, which is entirely good, through this all judgments and suffering are nullified, as mentioned above — which is what is needed on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, which is the <span class="h">yoma d'dina</span> [day of judgment], as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the <span class="h">shofar</span>. For when one is nullified there in the <span class="h">tachlis</span> due to the intensity of the judgment that is aroused then, as mentioned above, it is impossible to remain there, for one would be nullified from existence. And necessarily the <span class="h">bitul</span> must be in the aspect of <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span>, as mentioned above. And when one returns from the <span class="h">bitul</span>, the judgments wish to intensify even more etc., as mentioned above. And then one cools and nullifies the judgments through the Torah that one draws from the light of the shining of the <span class="h">bitul</span>, through the <span class="h">reshimah</span> that remains from the <span class="h">bitul</span> etc., as mentioned above. And through this one nullifies the judgments at the time of the return from the <span class="h">bitul</span>, as all this is explained in the Torah mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of <span class="h">shofar</span>. For the <span class="h">shofar</span> is the aspect of receiving the Torah, which was given with the sound of the <span class="h">shofar</span>. And so we mention the receiving of the Torah on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> in the <span class="h">berachah</span> of <span class="h">Shofros</span>, as we say: <span class="v">"You were revealed in a cloud of Your glory"</span> etc. For through the sound of the <span class="h">shofar</span> one arouses the aspect of the power of receiving the Torah — meaning the aspect of drawing the Torah that one draws from the light of the shining and sparkling of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> of the <span class="h">bitul</span>. And through this one nullifies the judgments at the time of the return from the <span class="h">bitul</span>, as mentioned above. Therefore it is explained in the <span class="h">seforim</span> that the essential sweetening of judgment on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> is through the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of the sound of the <span class="h">shofar</span>. For the essential sweetening of judgments is the sweetening that one sweetens them through the Torah that one draws from the <span class="h">reshimah</span> mentioned above at the time of the return from the <span class="h">bitul</span> — which is the aspect of the sound of the <span class="h">shofar</span>.</div>

<div class="p">For even though during the <span class="h">bitul</span> toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span> the judgments are completely nullified there, nevertheless the judgments are not yet sweetened in their place. For afterward, when one returns from the <span class="h">bitul</span>, the judgments return and intensify even more, as mentioned above. But the essential sweetening is when one merits to sweeten the judgments in their place and root — meaning when one returns from the <span class="h">bitul</span>, and there the judgments have the power to be aroused. And then they are aroused to intensify even more, because they see that one wants to nullify them through the <span class="h">bitul</span> — as is the way of two people who fight each other, that when one sees that the other is overpowering him, he intensifies even more, as explained in the Torah mentioned above. And then, when one merits to nullify the judgments through the Torah that one draws from the <span class="h">reshimah</span> mentioned above — which is the aspect of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of <span class="h">shofar</span>, as mentioned above — this is the essential sweetening of judgments, the aspect of sweetening judgments at their root and place. For in the aspect of the <span class="h">bitul</span>, the judgments are completely nullified there and have no hold at all. Their essential hold is only in the aspect of <span class="h">v'shov</span>, in the aspect of the return from the <span class="h">bitul</span> — there their hold begins. And when one nullifies them there, in their place, through the Torah drawn from the <span class="h">reshimah</span> as mentioned above — this is the essential aspect of sweetening judgments, as mentioned above. Therefore the essential sweetening of judgments on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> is through the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of <span class="h">shofar</span>, which is the aspect of the power of receiving the Torah that was given with the sound of the <span class="h">shofar</span>, which one draws on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> from the light of the shining of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> of the <span class="h">bitul</span>. Through this is the essential perfection of the sweetening of judgments in their place, as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 4                                   -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 4</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of why <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> is called <span class="h">Yom HaZikaron</span> [the Day of Remembrance]. For through all the above — meaning through the <span class="h">bitul</span> and through the Torah drawn from the <span class="h">reshimah</span> (which is the aspect of the <span class="h">shofar</span>) — through this one repairs prayer. For through this one draws the light of oneness, to know that it is entirely one. And through this one merits to make one unity out of the entire prayer: that even when standing at the end of the prayer one will still remember the first word of the prayer etc. This is the aspect of <span class="mx">"they forgot them and came back and re-established them,"</span> as explained there at the end of the Torah mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the <span class="h">nesirah</span> [severance/sawing apart] that is performed on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> through the sleep and <span class="h">dormita</span> that falls upon <span class="h">Ze'ir Anpin</span>, and through the <span class="h">shofar</span>, which is the awakening of the sleep — through which one severs and builds <span class="h">Malchus</span>, that it should be severed from the aspect of <span class="h">achor b'achor</span> [back to back] etc., as explained in the Kavanos. This is the aspect mentioned above, which is explained in the Torah mentioned above on the verse <span class="v">"Boaz said to Rus: Have you not heard, my daughter?"</span> etc. — that the utterance does not allow the soul to proceed from it etc. And this is caused by the fact that the light of oneness has not yet been drawn down in perfection, and there is still a hold of the <span class="h">klipos</span>, and they fear that when the soul proceeds further it will forget the first utterance. For the power of forgetfulness — which derives from the <span class="h">chitzonim</span> [external forces] — has the power to overpower. And then the Supernal <span class="h">Middos</span> [Attributes] are still in the aspect of <span class="h">achor b'achor</span>, as explained in the Kavanos — that as long as [the <span class="h">klipos</span>] have a hold, [the <span class="h">Middos</span>] must remain <span class="h">achor b'achor</span>. And then, because they are attached at a single wall to both of them, in the aspect of <span class="h">achor b'achor</span>, the utterance that is drawn from there does not allow the soul to proceed further. This is caused by the fact that above too they are grasped together, in the aspect of a single wall to both of them, in the aspect of <span class="h">achor b'achor</span>, from the concern of the hold of the <span class="h">klipos</span>, which are the aspect of forgetfulness.</div>

<div class="p">For the root of speech is from <span class="h">da'as</span>, as it is written: <span class="v">"from His mouth come knowledge and understanding."</span> But its emergence into the world is through the <span class="h">nefesh</span>, for the <span class="h">nefesh</span> brings forth and births all the utterances whose root is in <span class="h">da'as</span>. And as long as the light of oneness is not drawn down through the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above, the <span class="h">da'as</span> and the <span class="h">nefesh</span> — which are the aspects of <span class="h">Tiferes</span> and <span class="h">Malchus</span> — are grasped and attached together. And then the utterance does not allow the soul to proceed further, in the aspect of <span class="v">"Boaz said to Rus"</span> etc. Therefore a <span class="h">nesirah</span> is needed. And this is performed on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> through the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above and through the Torah drawn from the <span class="h">reshimah</span> etc. — which is the aspect of the <span class="h">shofar</span> etc., as mentioned above. Through this one draws the light of oneness, to know that it is entirely one. Through this one merits to make one unity out of the entire prayer, that even when standing at the last word of the prayer one will still remember the first word of the prayer. For forgetfulness has no dominion at all. And then the <span class="h">da'as</span> and the utterance allow the soul to proceed further, and they are no longer grasped and attached together out of the concern of forgetfulness, which is the <span class="h">klipos</span>. This is the aspect of the <span class="h">nesirah</span> mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> is called <span class="h">Yom HaZikaron</span> — the Day of Remembrance specifically. For then one draws remembrance. Through this one makes one unity out of the entire prayer, that even when standing at the end of the prayer one will still remember the first word of the prayer, as mentioned above. So that we can speak all the utterances of prayer and say afterward many more supplications and requests, as mentioned above. And this is what we need on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> and the Ten Days of <span class="h">Teshuvah</span>, when we need to multiply prayers and supplications and requests. Therefore we repair prayer then through the aspect of the remembrance mentioned above — which one draws through the <span class="h">bitul</span> and through the <span class="h">reshimah</span> that remains when returning from the <span class="h">bitul</span>, from where the receiving of the Torah derives, which is the aspect of the <span class="h">shofar</span> as mentioned above — through which one makes one unity out of the entire prayer. This is the essential tikun of prayer that we are engaged in repairing on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> in perfection.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 5                                   -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 5</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the <span class="h">teshuvah</span> that begins on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, which is the first day of the Ten Days of <span class="h">Teshuvah</span>. For the essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span> is through the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above. And this is the essential revelation of His G-dliness and <span class="h">Malchus</span> that we are engaged in revealing on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> — this is the essential aspect of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>: to reveal His G-dliness and <span class="h">Malchus</span> in the world, that all should know there is a sole Master who rules and governs His world as He wills. As we say in all the prayers: <span class="v">"and therefore, place Your dread"</span> etc.; <span class="v">"and You, Hashem, shall reign alone"</span> etc.; <span class="v">"reign over the entire world in Your glory"</span> etc.; <span class="v">"and every creature shall know that You made it, and every being shall understand that You formed it"</span> etc.</div>

<div class="p">For the essential revelation of His <span class="h">Malchus</span> and G-dliness is through the aspect of <span class="h">zikaron</span> [remembrance] — one must guard the remembrance exceedingly. And the essential aspect of the remembrance is to strive greatly that there should remain established in one's mind and <span class="h">da'as</span> and heart the light of the shining of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> from the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above — that one should remember well the sparkling and shining of this holy and awesome light, and never forget it. For the essential knowledge of His G-dliness and sovereignty is drawn through this light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span>. For Hashem is exalted and uplifted and lofty and elevated exceedingly beyond all worlds and all thoughts — for no thought grasps Him at all. And it is impossible to know of Him except through the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above — through nullifying oneself completely and totally from the vision of this world in the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span>. And then one merits to be included in Him. But at the time one is nullified there in the light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>, which is the <span class="h">tachlis</span> — this is not called knowledge. For there, it is above <span class="h">da'as</span>. As is explained elsewhere (<span class="lr">LM I:4</span>) on the verse: <span class="v">"and no man knows"</span> — even he himself does not know etc. The essential knowledge is when one returns from the <span class="h">bitul</span> and merits to draw Torah from the light of the sparkling of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> from the <span class="h">bitul</span> — this is the essential <span class="h">da'as</span>. And through this is the essential revelation of His G-dliness to all who come into the world.</div>

<div class="p">For most of the world does not merit the aspect of the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above. And their knowledge is only through the Torah that the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> draw for them from the <span class="h">reshimah</span> mentioned above. For the attainment of all the prophets and sages, and all the holy Torah that we received from the mouth of Moshe Rabbainu, peace upon him, and all the Torah that the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> of every generation draw — all of it is drawn from there, from the aspect of the light of the sparkling of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> of the <span class="h">bitul</span>, from which all the attainments of prophecy and <span class="h">ruach hakodesh</span> of all the true sages are drawn. For the essential knowledge of His G-dliness is from there, and it is the essential <span class="h">tachlis</span> of <span class="h">Olam HaBa</span> — in the aspect of: <span class="mx">"in the future, the Holy One blessed be He will make a dance for the <i>tzadikim</i>, and each one points with his finger"</span> etc. — as Rabbainu of blessed memory explained there in the Torah mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And the essential thing is the remembrance, as it is written: <span class="v">"only guard yourself and guard your soul exceedingly, lest you forget the things that your eyes saw — the day you stood before Hashem your G-d at Chorev"</span> etc. For because the light of the attainment of His G-dliness is so high and lofty and elevated, and no thought grasps it at all — only above thought, in the aspect of the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span> — there one merits that the light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> illuminates and shines upon him according to what one merits, fortunate is he. Therefore at the time of the return from the <span class="h">bitul</span>, one must guard the remembrance exceedingly — meaning one must sanctify the vessels of one's mind so they will be worthy vessels to receive the light of the shining of the <span class="h">reshimah</span>, in order to receive Torah from there — through which the essential attainment of His G-dliness derives, as mentioned above. For due to the greatness of the light, it cannot enter the vessels if they are not fit for this. And then, G-d forbid, this great light is forgotten from him. And this is the aspect of: <span class="v">"lest you forget the things that your eyes saw."</span> For even though at the time of the <span class="h">bitul</span> one saw with one's own eyes what one saw, nevertheless afterward one can easily forget what one saw with one's own eyes. Therefore the verse warns exceedingly about this — to guard the remembrance. Meaning: one must sanctify the vessels of one's mind and <span class="h">da'as</span>, so that one can receive Torah through the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span>, so that one will remember well the light of the shining and sparkling of the <span class="h">bitul</span> — through which the essential attainment of His G-dliness derives, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, which is called <span class="h">Yom HaZikaron</span> — the Day of Remembrance specifically. For the essential revelation of His G-dliness that one draws on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, as mentioned above, is through the aspect of remembrance, as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 6                                   -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 6</div>

<div class="p">And the essential guarding of the remembrance is through the perfection of the speech of prayer that one draws on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> — meaning the making of one unity out of the entire prayer, through which one can speak many prayers and supplications and requests. Through this, the remembrance is guarded, in the aspect of: <span class="v">"for whenever I speak of him, I surely remember him still"</span> etc. For due to the greatness and awesomeness of the light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> that one attains during the <span class="h">bitul</span>, it is impossible to receive it in any vessel. And even the light of the sparkling and shining of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> that remains from the <span class="h">bitul</span> when it is in the aspect of <span class="h">v'shov</span> — also, it is impossible to receive it except through many tikunim, which are the aspect of many holy and awesome vessels through which one receives the light, in order that we can receive the attainment of His G-dliness. For one needs very many vessels in order to receive through them the light of the sparkling and shining of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> of the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one needs for this many, many letters and utterances — more than the mouth can speak and the heart can think. All of them are the aspect of vessels and contractions through which to receive the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> of the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above. And this is the aspect of the entirety of the holy Torah, which is comprised of twenty-two letters and five doubled final letters [<span class="h">mantzepach</span>]. These combine and revolve in many, many combinations, until they were combined in these utterances of the entirety of the holy Torah — all of which are the aspect of vessels and contractions through which to receive the light of the shining of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> mentioned above, from which the entire Torah is drawn, as mentioned above. Therefore the Torah has no perfection unless it contains all the letters and words and sections, and if one letter is missing from a Sefer Torah, it is <span class="h">pasul</span> [unfit]. For Hashem calculated in His <span class="h">da'as</span> that it is impossible to receive the light of the shining of the revelation of His G-dliness — which is drawn through the <span class="h">reshimah</span> mentioned above — except through all of these vessels specifically, in their exact form and image, which are all the letters, words and sections of the holy Torah.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of all the 613 <span class="h">mitzvos</span> and all the Rabbinic <span class="h">mitzvos</span>. For all of them are the aspect of vessels and contractions through which to receive the light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> drawn through the shining of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> mentioned above. Therefore one must fulfill each <span class="h">mitzvah</span> according to its laws, in all its details and fine points, as our Sages of blessed memory received. For Hashem calculated in His <span class="h">da'as</span> that it is impossible to draw the revelation of His G-dliness in the world except through these vessels specifically, made through these <span class="h">mitzvos</span> specifically, according to their laws and details. Therefore one must speak many utterances in Torah and prayer, day and night, and not be silent and not be still and not give oneself rest. For one needs very, very many holy utterances in order to form through them holy vessels, in order to merit receiving the light of the sparkling and shining mentioned above, in order to merit recognizing the Creator, may His Name be blessed.</div>

<div class="p">It emerges that through the remembrance itself that we draw on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> — meaning through the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above and through the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of <span class="h">shofar</span>, through which one draws the aspect of the Torah from the <span class="h">reshimah</span> etc. as mentioned above — through which one draws the light of oneness, through which one makes one unity out of the entire prayer (this being the aspect of remembrance — meaning that even when standing at the end, at the last word of the prayer, one is still standing at the first word, for one still remembers the first word of the prayer, as mentioned above) — through which one can speak many utterances etc. as mentioned above — through this itself the remembrance is guarded. Meaning: one will remember His G-dliness and never forget Him. For the more holy utterances one speaks, the more one draws the revelation of His G-dliness, and the more one merits to remember Him, as mentioned above. And all this we repair on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>. Therefore <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> is called <span class="h">Yom HaZikaron</span>. For the essential thing on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> is to reveal His G-dliness — which is drawn through the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> of the <span class="h">bitul</span>, as mentioned above. And the essential thing is the remembrance: that one should merit to bind well the light of the sparkling and shining into one's mind and heart, thoroughly, in a strong and firm bond — that one should not forget the things that one's eyes saw.</div>

<div class="p">For most people, especially those who have begun to enter somewhat into the service of Hashem, generally it happens that some sparkling from Hashem comes upon them, and then their hearts burn exceedingly for Hashem with expiry of soul. But afterward, the light of this sparkling passes from their minds, and then they forget completely, as if they never saw lights in their lives. Therefore one needs great strengthening to constantly remind oneself. And to bind in one's <span class="h">da'as</span> and heart thoroughly and well, in a strong and firm bond, all the sparklings and shinings that Hashem has shone upon him in the days of old, from the day of his birth until this day. In order to merit strengthening in His service to draw upon oneself the revelation of His G-dliness (as is explained regarding this in <span class="lr">LM I:222</span>). And this is the essential aspect of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, which is called <span class="h">Yom HaZikaron</span> — the Day of Remembrance specifically. For then is the essential drawing of the revelation of His G-dliness through the <span class="h">reshimah</span> mentioned above — which is the aspect of remembrance, as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 7                                   -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 7</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the <span class="h">teshuvah</span> that begins on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, which is the first day of the Ten Days of <span class="h">Teshuvah</span>. For the essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span> is through the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above — which is the aspect of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of all the fasts and mortifications that are decreed upon penitents, each according to their sin. For one must accept upon oneself fasts and suffering, in order to close one's eyes from the vision of this world completely, in order to merit through this the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> — which is the essential aspect of <span class="h">teshuvah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">For in truth, every person must give up their life for the sake of Hashem and close their eyes from the vision of this world completely — meaning one must break all the desires completely and accept upon oneself all the afflictions and mortifications for the sake of Hashem, like R' Akiva whose flesh was raked with iron combs until his soul departed with <span class="v">"Echad."</span> And then specifically one will merit to attain His G-dliness in the ultimate attainment — fortunate is the one who merits this. But not every person is able to withstand this, to break everything and nullify oneself entirely. Therefore Hashem had compassion on us and gave us His Torah, which is <span class="v">"a tree of life to those who hold it"</span> etc., and <span class="v">"its ways are ways of pleasantness."</span> Meaning: through the Torah one merits to recognize Him by fulfilling all the <span class="h">mitzvos</span> and engaging in Torah all one's days. For then one can merit eternal life even without having broken all desires in perfection.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of what our Sages of blessed memory said on the verse <span class="v">"Bereishis — G-d created"</span> etc.: <span class="mx">"At first it arose in thought to create the world with the attribute of strict judgment. He saw that the world could not endure, so He arose and joined the attribute of mercy with the attribute of judgment."</span> At first glance this is puzzling — for it is known and explained in all the <span class="h">seforim</span> that the essential purpose of creation was on account of His compassion and goodness, to reveal His goodness and mercy to the world. And seemingly this contradicts the statement above that at first it arose in thought to create the world with strict judgment. But in truth, everything is well-understood based on the above. For Hashem, from the greatness of His mercy and goodness, wanting to bestow good upon His creatures with the ultimate good of all goods, therefore it arose in thought at first to create the world with the attribute of strict judgment — meaning that the world should be governed through strict judgment, so that through the intensity of the judgment, the world would nullify themselves to Him in the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span>. As explained above: through the intensity of judgments and suffering, one nullifies oneself completely in order to gaze upon and be included in the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, where suffering and judgments are nullified, as mentioned above — which is the aspect of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, as mentioned above. And then they would merit the perfection of the supreme <span class="h">tachlis</span>: to attain Him in an immensely awesome attainment that one merits through the true <span class="h">bitul</span>, which is drawn specifically through the attribute of strict judgment, as mentioned above. And for this purpose it arose in thought to create the world with strict judgment. For this was exceedingly great compassion — in order to merit through this specifically the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">But He saw that the world could not endure, for most of the world is unable to withstand this — to nullify themselves completely from this world. And even when the attribute of judgment comes upon them, G-d forbid, they do not merit to nullify themselves completely in truth as is proper. As it is written (Iyov 36): <span class="v">"the flatterers of heart store up wrath; they do not cry out when He binds them."</span> And as it is written: <span class="v">"it scorched him all around and he did not know; it burned in him and he did not take it to heart."</span> For what Rabbainu of blessed memory wrote there in the Torah mentioned above — that it is embedded in human nature to close one's eyes forcefully during severe suffering, and this derives from the aspect of the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, which is specifically through closing the eyes etc. as explained there — this is merely a faint reflection of the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above, for a brief moment. And it derives from the hidden wonders of His governance, from which the nature mentioned above is drawn, as explained there. But to truly merit through suffering and judgment, G-d forbid, the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above in full, until one attains the good <span class="h">tachlis</span> through it etc. as mentioned above — this not every person merits. As is explained there: this is the sign whether one has accomplished a good work through the suffering — if one merits to attain Torah through the suffering etc. And this is what is written: <span class="v">"fortunate is the man whom Y-ah chastens, and from Your Torah You teach him"</span> etc. — see there. It emerges that it is explained there, in its place, that not every person merits through suffering the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above in perfection and as is proper — only <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and the upright, fortunate are they. In the aspect of: <span class="v">"fortunate is the man whom [He] chastens"</span> etc. — <span class="v">"fortunate"</span> specifically. For not every person merits this. And every person must strengthen himself exceedingly, and beseech and plead greatly from Hashem that he should merit through suffering to nullify himself to Hashem in the aspect mentioned above, until one merits to attain through them etc. as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">On account of this, Hashem saw that the world could not endure through the attribute of judgment. For even though Hashem's intention in wanting to create the world with the attribute of judgment was for exceedingly great good, as mentioned above — and <span class="v">"G-d intended it for good"</span> — nevertheless He saw that the world could not endure with this. For most of the world is unable to withstand and endure this — to merit the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above through judgments and suffering, as mentioned above. And when one does not merit the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above, certainly it is impossible to endure if the world were governed through the attribute of strict judgment alone, G-d forbid. Therefore He placed the attribute of mercy first and joined it with the attribute of judgment.</div>

<div class="p">Meaning: in His immense mercy He devised plans so that no one would be banished from Him. Therefore He thought at first to create with strict judgment, and afterward joined the attribute of mercy with the attribute of judgment — so that through this two aspects and levels would be drawn, so that the world could endure, and all would merit the eternal <span class="h">tachlis</span>. For in His immense mercy, for the sake of the world's endurance, He joined mercy with judgment, that the world should be governed and endure through both. Meaning: the world should be governed and endure through the holy Torah, which is comprised of both — right and left, <span class="h">chesed</span> and judgment — for the Torah is the middle pillar. Meaning: the world should be governed through the holy Torah. For the holy Torah is entirely holy counsels — the aspect of the 613 counsels of the Torah. Through meriting to fulfill the Torah — to fulfill each <span class="h">mitzvah</span> in simplicity according to its laws as our Sages of blessed memory received — through this one can merit to draw upon oneself holiness and purity, as we say: <span class="v">"who has sanctified us with His <i>mitzvos</i>."</span> Through this one merits to recognize Him through the light of the holy <span class="h">reshimah</span> mentioned above, from which the entire holy Torah is drawn, as mentioned above — even if one has not yet merited the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above in perfection, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And also through fulfilling the Torah and <span class="h">mitzvos</span> in their plain sense, one draws upon oneself holiness and purity each time, through each <span class="h">mitzvah</span> and through each word of Torah, prayer and fear of Heaven. Until through this one merits to ascend from level to level, until one's heart is aroused to nullify oneself completely and totally to Hashem, to close one's eyes from the vision of this world completely, until one merits the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above in perfection, as mentioned above. For the Torah — <span class="v">"its ways are ways of pleasantness"</span> — and He did not burden us with any <span class="h">mitzvah</span>, as it is written: <span class="v">"the Almighty — we cannot find Him of great power"</span> etc. — as Rashi explains there: that He did not exhaust His creatures excessively, but only with mercy etc. — a handful of <span class="h">Minchah</span>, half a shekel, doves and young pigeons, cattle and sheep. And He did not trouble [us] to seek wild oxen and wild donkeys etc. — see there in Rashi (Iyov 37). And all the <span class="h">mitzvos</span> are light and pleasant to perform. And the lightest of light pursuits of this world is heavier and comes with more toil and effort than the greatest of great <span class="h">mitzvos</span>. For the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of <span class="h">tzitzis</span>, which is one of the great <span class="h">mitzvos</span>, is purchased for a single coin. And similarly <span class="h">tefillin</span>: one need not cross seas and deserts to find them — one simply purchases them for a small sum. And so with all the other <span class="h">mitzvos</span>. As it is written: <span class="v">"for this <i>mitzvah</i> is not too wondrous for you, nor is it far away. It is not in heaven... nor is it beyond the sea... for the matter is very close to you"</span> etc.</div>

<div class="p">And as we heard once from his holy conversations — he said with his holy mouth, in the grace, sweetness and pleasantness of his holy and exceedingly, exceedingly awesome conversation, endlessly and without limit: that the greatest of all great works in the service of Hashem is far lighter to perform than a small pursuit that one exerts oneself with for livelihood and worldly affairs. For do we not see how many exertions a person toils with in order to travel to some market fair? Immediately on <span class="h">Motzai Shabbos</span> one must toil and search for a wagon, and one rushes greatly to load one's merchandise onto the wagon with great effort. And afterward one must travel the entire night, banishing sleep from one's eyes. And one's entire body is broken because one must sit on a loaded wagon — and other toils and efforts of that sort, as is known to the world. And afterward one must stand all day on one's feet etc., especially during times of intense cold etc. And all these heavy and difficult exertions and toils — one exerts oneself for a doubt. For it is doubtful whether one will earn some small profit, and doubtful whether one will lose the expenses, as is known to the world. It emerges that for worldly livelihood one exerts oneself so much with such efforts for a doubtful small profit. While the greatest of all great works of service is prayer — and even so, when one stands to pray, one prays and completes the prayer. It emerges that through the holy Torah, every person can merit the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of: He saw that the world could not endure — He arose and joined the attribute of mercy with the attribute of judgment. Meaning: the world should be governed through the Torah, which is drawn from the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> mentioned above — which is the aspect of joining mercy with judgment. For the Torah is comprised of both, as mentioned above. But there are great and exceedingly awesome <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who sanctify themselves exceedingly, and nullify themselves completely, and close their eyes from the vision of this world completely — until they truly merit the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above in perfection. And they truly merit to ascend to the first thought, and to merit the supreme <span class="h">tachlis</span>, and they attain what they attain — fortunate are they. And this conduct is drawn from the aspect of what arose in thought at first — to create the world with the attribute of strict judgment — through which one merits the <span class="h">tachlis</span> of the <span class="h">bitul</span> in perfection. This is the aspect of the level of the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span>. And such <span class="h">tzadikim</span> are called <span class="h">chasidim</span>, in the aspect of: <span class="mx">"who is a <i>chasid</i>? One who acts with loving-kindness toward his Maker."</span> They perform <span class="h">chesed</span> with their Maker and sanctify themselves with extra holiness, and accept upon themselves all the harsh and bitter suffering and afflictions for the sake of Hashem — what they were not commanded to do at all. And they truly merit the supreme <span class="h">tachlis</span> etc., as mentioned above. For these <span class="h">tzadikim</span> conduct themselves according to the governance that arose in thought at first — to create the world with the attribute of strict judgment. For in the greatness of their power they are able to endure according to this governance. Therefore they truly merit what they merit — fortunate are they.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 8                                   -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 8</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of what our Sages of blessed memory said: that Moshe Rabbainu, peace upon him, asked about the death of R' Akiva — when he saw that they were raking his flesh with iron combs — <span class="mx">"this is Torah and this is its reward?"</span> Hashem said to him: <span class="mx">"be silent — thus it arose in thought."</span> The meaning is as mentioned above: that Moshe asked <span class="mx">"this is Torah and this is its reward?"</span> — <span class="v">"this is Torah"</span> specifically. For according to the governance through the holy Torah — which is the aspect of joining mercy with judgment — certainly such harsh judgments should not befall R' Akiva, to rake his flesh etc. And this is: <span class="mx">"this is Torah and this is its reward"</span> — <span class="v">"this is Torah and this is its reward"</span> specifically, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">Hashem answered him: <span class="mx">"be silent — thus it arose in thought."</span> <span class="v">"Thus it arose in thought"</span> specifically. For R' Akiva, in the greatness of his piety and his immense holiness, merited to ascend into the first thought. Meaning: he merited the supreme <span class="h">tachlis</span> that those who merit it do, which is the aspect of the <span class="h">tachlis</span> that one merits through the governance that arose in thought at first — to create the world with the attribute of strict judgment — from which are drawn all the harsh judgments and suffering of the awesome <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, whom Hashem examines as finely as a hair's breadth. For they merit the aspect of the governance mentioned above that arose in thought at first. Through this they merit the supreme <span class="h">tachlis</span>, the highest of the high, and they attain what they attain. For they merit through the judgments and suffering the aspect of the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above in perfection, as mentioned above. And this is: <span class="mx">"be silent — thus it arose in thought"</span> — <span class="v">"thus it arose in thought"</span> specifically, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is: <span class="v">"G-d saw the light, that it was good, and He separated it"</span> etc. And our Sages of blessed memory expounded: <span class="mx">"He saw the light, that it was good, and that the world was not worthy to use it. He arose and hid it for the <i>tzadikim</i> for the future."</span> The meaning is as mentioned above. For the light that illuminated during the seven days of creation — with which a person could see from one end of the world to the other — this light is drawn from the aspect of the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above toward <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>, for one who merits this in perfection. Therefore Hashem saw that the world was not worthy to use it — meaning as mentioned above: He saw that the world does not merit to nullify themselves so much that they would be fit to receive this great and awesome light. And when they are not fit to receive it, then it becomes the aspect of excessive light, G-d forbid, which causes, G-d forbid, the Breaking of Vessels — from which the hold of all the <span class="h">klipos</span> derives, as is known. Therefore He diminished the light and did not draw to this world except a small and gradual light drawn from the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> mentioned above, as mentioned above — from which the light of the Torah derives, which currently clothes itself in these garments so that we can receive it. But the light of the seven days of creation — which is the light of the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above — He arose and hid it for the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> for the future. Meaning: there are great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who nullify themselves in complete <span class="h">bitul</span>. For they close their eyes from the vision of this world completely etc. as mentioned above. And they truly merit this hidden light, which is the light of the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above, in a wondrous illumination, as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 9                                   -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 9</div>

<div class="p">It emerges that the Torah is the path of life etc. — through which one merits to recognize the Creator, even if one has not yet merited the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above in perfection, as mentioned above. And also through the holy Torah one can ascend from level to level, until one's heart is aroused to sanctify oneself with extra holiness, and to close one's eyes from the vision of this world completely, until one comes to the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above. For the Torah is <span class="v">"the path of life, the rebuke of instruction,"</span> and <span class="v">"its ways are ways of pleasantness"</span> etc. And it does not burden us with anything — <span class="mx">"whatever He forbade us, He permitted us"</span> etc. — as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">But when a person, G-d forbid, transgresses some matter of Torah, he damages the channel and vessel through which one receives the light of the revelation of His G-dliness from the <span class="h">reshimah</span> mentioned above. And if so, how is it possible to repair this? One must repair it through <span class="h">teshuvah</span>. And <span class="h">teshuvah</span> is higher than Torah — meaning: because one transgressed the Torah, one must do <span class="h">teshuvah</span>. And <span class="h">teshuvah</span> means closing one's eyes from the vision of this world completely. And this is the aspect of the fasts and mortifications that are decreed upon penitents. For because one transgressed the words of Torah, one must ascend to the aspect of complete <span class="h">bitul</span> — to accept upon oneself fasts and mortifications and close one's eyes from the vision of this world completely, until one merits to come to the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above, and to gaze upon the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, which is entirely good, entirely one.</div>

<div class="p">And there, in the aspect of the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above, it is above Torah. Therefore there specifically one can repair what one damaged and ruined by transgressing the Torah. For there, it is above Torah — for the entire Torah is drawn from there. Therefore one can draw from there lights and tikunim to repair what one damaged in matters of Torah, as mentioned above. Therefore our Sages of blessed memory said: <span class="mx">"in the place where penitents stand, even the completely righteous cannot stand."</span> For one who fulfills the Torah in simplicity is called a completely righteous person. But penitents have ascended to the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, which is above Torah — from which the entire Torah is drawn. This is the aspect of the hidden light from the seven days of creation, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, where the essential beginning of <span class="h">teshuvah</span> is. For it is the first day of the Ten Days of <span class="h">Teshuvah</span>. For on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> one ascends to the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above, as mentioned above. And this is the essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, as mentioned above.</div>

