<div class="ssn">Halachah 4 Overview</div>

<div class="p">This is the longest single halachah in all of Tefilas Arvis — 39 osos — expounding Rosh HaShanah, the Ten Days, Yom Kippur, Sukkos, the Four Species, Hoshana Rabbah, Shemini Atzeres and Simchas Torah, all based on LM I:65 ("Vayomer Boaz el Rus"). Part 1 covers §§1-9, laying the conceptual foundation.</div>

<div class="ssn">§§ 1–2: Arvis = Bitul = Perfection of Prayer</div>

<div class="p">Arvis is the first of the three daily prayers (night precedes day). Its essential tikun: bitul — closing one's eyes from "the vision of this world" completely, being included in the tachlis ("entirely one, entirely good"). At that level, all suffering is nullified. Through this bitul, one achieves the perfection of prayer: making one unity out of the entire prayer — standing at the last word and still remembering the first. This is accomplished through the Ba'al HaSadeh (Master of the Field) who tends the Supernal Field. The Ari"zal's instruction: during Arvis, close one's eyes from beginning to end. At night, judgments intensify — the bitul of Arvis nullifies them and repairs prayer.</div>

<div class="ssn">§§ 3–4: Rosh HaShanah = the Bitul; Shofar = Torah from the Reshimah</div>

<div class="p">Rosh HaShanah = the great Day of Judgment = the dormita (deep slumber upon Ze'ir Anpin) = bitul. The shofar = receiving Torah from the reshimah (trace) that remains when one returns from the bitul. During the bitul, judgments are completely nullified — but this is not yet sweetening "in their place." When one returns (v'shov), judgments intensify more fiercely (like two fighters). The essential sweetening: drawing Torah from the reshimah upon return, which is the mitzvah of shofar (Torah given with the shofar's sound). This sweetens judgments at their root. Rosh HaShanah = Yom HaZikaron (Remembrance): through the bitul + shofar, one draws remembrance → one can make the prayer into a unity → the nesirah (Tiferes and Malchus severed from back-to-back) is accomplished.</div>

<div class="ssn">§§ 5–6: Remembrance = Revelation of G-dliness; the Torah as Vessels</div>

<div class="p">Teshuvah begins on Rosh HaShanah. The essential purpose: revealing His Malchus so all know there is a sole Master. This revelation is through the reshimah — but the light is so great it must be received through vessels. The Torah's 22+5 letters in all their combinations = the vessels/contractions needed to receive the light. Every mitzvah = a vessel. Every letter of Torah and prayer = a vessel. If even one letter is missing from a Sefer Torah, it is pasul. The more holy utterances spoken, the more G-dliness is revealed, the stronger the remembrance. Most people experience sparkling moments from Hashem — but then forget. One must bind every previous sparkling into memory. This is Yom HaZikaron.</div>

<div class="ssn">§§ 7–8: Two Paths — Torah (Mercy + Judgment) vs. Bitul (Strict Judgment)</div>

<div class="p">God originally intended to create the world with strict judgment alone — so that through intense suffering people would reach ultimate bitul and attain the supreme tachlis. But He saw the world couldn't endure, so He joined mercy with judgment = the Torah. The Torah's "ways are ways of pleasantness" — every mitzvah is lighter than any worldly pursuit. (R' Nosson records Rabbainu's holy conversation about how traveling to a market fair is more exhausting than prayer.) Through Torah one ascends gradually to bitul. But great tzadikim (chasidim) operate on the original plan of strict judgment — they sanctify themselves beyond what is required, accepting suffering voluntarily, and merit the supreme tachlis directly. R' Akiva's martyrdom: "this is Torah and this is its reward?" — "Be silent — thus it arose in thought" = he merited the first thought, the governance of strict judgment. The hidden light of the seven days of creation = the light of the bitul, hidden for tzadikim.</div>

<div class="ssn">§ 9: Teshuvah = Above Torah</div>

<div class="p">Torah is the path of life for all. But when one transgresses Torah, one must do teshuvah — which is above Torah. Through fasts and mortifications one reaches the bitul, which is the source from which Torah itself is drawn. There one can draw lights to repair what was damaged. "In the place where penitents stand, the completely righteous cannot stand" — for penitents access the root above Torah. This is Rosh HaShanah: beginning of teshuvah = ascending to bitul.</div>

